Ask questions about text and use the answers in scripts.

Usage:
  onesie [question] [flags]
  onesie [command]

Available Commands:
  cache       Report what the response cache holds
  calibrate   Score questions against records whose answers are known
  completion  Generate the autocompletion script for the specified shell
  help        Help about any command
  profile     Create, inspect and remove the named connections a run uses
  questions   List the question files -f finds by name
  version     Print version, commit, build date and the built in limits

Asking

Flags:
      --ask NAME=QUESTION       Ask a named question. Repeat for several.
      --pick OPTIONS            Choose from comma separated options.
      --rate LEVELS             Rate against levels from lowest to highest.
      --desc KEY=TEXT           Describe an option, level, yes or no.
  -f, --file FILE               Load saved questions, a request body or a built in
                                starter set.
      --print-questions         Write a question file to stdout and exit. Its first line
                                names the schema --print-schema prints.
      --print-schema            Write the JSON Schema for a question file to stdout and
                                exit.
      --for ID                  Select a question from -f by ID, so the question flags
                                after it apply to that question, each with a notice.
                                Repeatable.
      --sep SEP                 Set the separator for the --pick and --rate that follow
                                it.
      --box                     Make this a box question, answered with the left, top,
                                right and bottom edges of what it finds in an image,
                                each from 0 to 1. Takes no value. Experimental on
                                TypeSafe's side.
      --replace                 Let --ask override an id from -f.

How they work together:
  A positional question has the id answer. By default a question asks for a yes or no
  probability. Use --pick for a choice, --rate for ordered levels, or --box for image
  edges. A rate score is the level index averaged over p, from 0, and norm is score over
  the top index, from 0 to 1. Box edges are left, top, right and bottom, each from 0 to
  1. Box is experimental on TypeSafe's side. See Input for image restrictions.

  Question flags apply to the preceding --ask or --for. Flags before any group apply to
  the one question asked, whether positional, a lone --ask or a file with one question.
  Beside several questions they need --for ID. --ask NAME=QUESTION opens a named group
  and may repeat. --for ID selects a question from --file. Root runs issue a notice for
  each setting overriding a selected file question. Repeated --pick adds options and
  repeated --rate adds levels after earlier ones. Together they replace the file
  question's options or levels. --desc KEY=TEXT replaces only that option, level, yes or
  no description. A rate needs every level described or none. --sep changes the
  separator for following --pick and --rate. --for may repeat and keeps the wording and
  settings that no flag changes. It needs a file and an existing id and refuses
  --replace. Flags cannot change a file question shape.

  --file accepts a question file, a request body, a path or a saved name. Saved names
  are looked up in .onesie/questions, then the config directory, then built in starter
  sets. --ask needs --replace to replace an id from the file. This replaces the
  question, while --for changes selected settings.

  A question that begins with a dash needs -- before it, with all flags first, as in
  onesie -o json -- '-is this urgent'. Use the same escape for a one word question close
  to a command name, since onesie questoins exits 2 and suggests questions.
  --print-questions exports a question file whose first line names the schema from
  --print-schema. Exports send no requests. On a request body, --for accepts only
  threshold, minimum confidence and fallback settings.

Input

Flags:
      --state TEXT              Read this text instead of stdin. Use - to read stdin.
      --state-file FILE         Read the state from a file.
  -i, --input FORMAT            One of text, json, jsonl, lines, csv, tsv or request.
                                Default: text.
      --map EXPR                Select the content to evaluate with a jq expression.
      --delimiter CHAR          Set the field separator -i csv reads and -o csv writes:
                                auto, which needs -i csv and writes the one it detects,
                                or one character. Defaults to a comma.
      --image NAME=PATH         Put a PNG or JPEG in the state under NAME. Repeatable.
                                For one record stdin is read beside it only through
                                --state -, so text joins it from --state, --state-file
                                or --state -. A stream reads its records from stdin and
                                adds the image to each. Experimental on TypeSafe's side.
      --image-field NAME        Read a PNG or JPEG per record from the path this state
                                key holds. Streaming only, repeatable. Under -i lines
                                the line is the path. Experimental on TypeSafe's side.
      --id EXPR                 Name each record on every output line with the string or
                                number this jq expression returns. Streaming only. It
                                runs one record at a time as the input is read, so keep
                                it cheap, such as a field lookup. -V lists the cap on
                                its length.
      --skip-blank              Drop blank lines with no output line. Streaming only.

How they work together:
  Text and JSON read one state. JSONL, lines, CSV and TSV stream records from stdin.
  Request input sends API shaped bodies as written. --state supplies text instead of
  stdin, --state-file reads a file, and --state - explicitly reads stdin. --map runs a
  jq expression on each original record and sends its result as the state. Map only the
  text a person would read. Object keys come out sorted. The result may nest
  max-map-depth levels deep and encode to max-line-bytes, as version lists. The original
  record remains available for merged output.

  --id runs one record at a time as input is read. Its result must be a string or
  number. Keep it cheap, such as a field lookup. version lists max-id-bytes.
  --skip-blank drops blank lines without emitting a result. The default field separator
  is ','. --delimiter auto needs CSV input, detects its separator and uses it for CSV
  output. An explicit delimiter takes one character. TSV uses tabs.

  --image NAME=PATH adds a PNG or JPEG under NAME. For one record, stdin is read beside
  an image only through --state -. Text can also join it through --state or
  --state-file. Images cannot be mixed with request input. Streams read stdin and add
  the fixed image to every record. --image-field reads a PNG or JPEG path from a state
  key per record. Under lines input the line itself is the path. Image support is
  experimental on the TypeSafe side. See version for image, file, token and model
  limits.

Decisions

Flags:
      --threshold NUMBER        Turn a yes or no probability into a boolean decision.
      --assert EXPR             Exit 1 when an answer fails this condition.
  -q, --quiet                   Suppress output. Without --assert, judge one answer.
      --min-confidence NUMBER   The confidence a pick or rate answer must reach. Needs
                                --fallback. Confidence is about 1 when p sits on one
                                answer.
      --fallback VALUE          Use this as answer.decision on low confidence or error.
                                JSON keeps the model answer in answer.value when
                                available.
      --abstain-if EXPR         Turn a no into an unsure when this condition holds.
                                Repeatable, combined with and. Needs --assert and
                                replaces the -f file's abstain_if.

How they work together:
  --threshold adds a boolean decision to a yes or no probability. A false decision alone
  does not change the exit code. Without --assert, quiet mode judges one question on one
  record. Yes or no uses the threshold, defaulting to 0.5. Pick and rate need both
  --min-confidence and --fallback. Quiet mode refuses streams.

  --min-confidence applies only to pick and rate, needs --fallback and takes a value
  between 0 and 1. Confidence is about 1 when p sits on one answer. Yes or no fallback
  values must be boolean spellings. Pick and rate can fall back to a review destination
  outside their options. JSON retains the model answer in answer.value when available,
  with the policy result in answer.decision. Low confidence has fallback reason
  low_confidence and an error has reason error. A fallback does not turn an error into
  an answer.

  Each record ends in one verdict: answered with no gate, pass, fail, unsure or error.
  --assert is a boolean expression over the record. Repeated assertions are combined
  with and and replace the file's assert. Acceptance is checked first. Only when it
  fails is --abstain-if checked. Repeated abstain expressions combine with and, need
  --assert and replace the file's abstain_if. A false assertion is a fail and exits 1.
  When --abstain-if holds instead, the record is unsure and exits 7. Quiet beside
  --assert only suppresses output.

  An --assert path is a question id, a dot and a field, as in urgent.value. Compare with
  ==, !=, <, <=, > and >=, test a string with in ["a", "b"], and combine with and, or,
  not and parentheses. Strings take double quotes. An id written bare holds only
  letters, digits and _, starts with a letter or _, and is not and, or, not, in, true or
  false. Write any other id in brackets and double quotes, as in ["needs review"].value,
  and an option the same way after p, as in team.p["on call"]. min, max, sum and avg
  take numbers and nest, as in max(a.value, b.value) < 0.2. There are no arithmetic
  operators, so weight answers with jq. A box has no value, so gate it on its edges or
  its confidence, as in mug.confidence >= 0.6.

  Streams print every fail and unsure too. --stop-on=fail prints the first fail before
  stopping, and --stop-on=unsure the first unsure. Both need --assert, and unsure also
  needs --abstain-if. Earlier errors still affect the aggregate exit code. See Asking
  for question setting scope. Use onesie help calibrate to choose cuts from labelled
  evidence. The export example uses an illustrative cut.

  Inspect a policy without asking the model:
    onesie --ask 'urgent=is this urgent' --threshold 0.5 --print-questions

Output

Flags:
  -o, --output FORMAT           One of auto, json, values, table, raw, csv, tsv or
                                markdown.
      --merge                   Keep input fields and attach the answers.
  -V, --version                 Print the version, commit, build date and built in
                                limits, as one JSON object under -o json.
      --out FILE                Write the answers to this file rather than stdout.
      --merge-key KEY           Put the answers under this key in the merged record.
                                Implies --merge.
      --usage                   Add the API usage object to JSON output.
      --stats                   Write a one line summary of the run to stderr at exit.
  -h, --help                    Show this help.

How they work together:
  Auto uses a table only for one result on a terminal, without --merge or --out. It uses
  JSON when redirected, for streams, for merged records and for --out files. -o raw
  prints the policy decision when present, otherwise the answer value, and does not
  preserve the original record.

  --merge keeps the input fields and places answer metadata under answers by default.
  --merge-key selects another location and implies --merge. JSON preserves model values,
  decisions and errors. A request error remains in the output even when a fallback
  supplies a decision. --out writes answers to a file instead of stdout. See Execution
  for resume and pruning. --usage adds API usage to JSON output. --stats writes a one
  line run summary to stderr at exit. --version prints the version, commit, build date
  and built in limits, as one JSON object with -o json.

Connections

Flags:
      --list-models             Write the available models to stdout and exit.
      --provider PROVIDER       Run on this provider without a profile: typesafe,
                                openrouter, berget, levanto or cloudflare. Overrides
                                ONESIE_PROVIDER and the default profile. Refused beside
                                --profile or ONESIE_PROFILE.
      --profile NAME            The profile a run uses, from profiles.json in the config
                                dir. Overrides ONESIE_PROFILE and ONESIE_PROVIDER.
                                Otherwise the default profile runs, which onesie profile
                                use sets.
  -m, --model MODEL             Use this model.
      --base-url URL            Use this API root. Also ONESIE_BASE_URL.
      --allow-http              Send the request, and any API key, over a plain http
                                base URL to a host other than this machine. Without it
                                such a base URL exits 2 before any request.

How they work together:
  onesie is a Unix filter over the System One API, served by typesafe, openrouter,
  berget, levanto or cloudflare. A profile in profiles.json holds a provider, a key in
  the OS keychain or file, and optionally a model and base URL or cloudflare account.
  onesie profile create stores one and onesie profile use makes it the default.

  A run uses the profile named by --profile, then ONESIE_PROFILE, then the default
  profile. --profile overrides ONESIE_PROVIDER. --provider runs without a profile and
  overrides ONESIE_PROVIDER and the default profile. It is refused beside --profile or
  ONESIE_PROFILE. ONESIE_PROVIDER beside ONESIE_PROFILE is also refused. With no
  profiles, typesafe runs directly. Provider keys come from TYPESAFE_API_KEY,
  OPENROUTER_API_KEY, BERGET_API_KEY, LEVANTO_API_KEY or CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN. The
  chosen profile key and endpoint beat the environment. Provider variables beat the
  default profile. --base-url wins over a chosen profile, then ONESIE_BASE_URL, then the
  provider base URL or account variable, then the default profile. On typesafe,
  ONESIE_BASE_URL beats TYPESAFE_BASE_URL.

  A key goes only with its own endpoint. A custom profile endpoint takes only the key
  from that profile. A custom URL from a variable takes only the provider key variable.
  A typed --base-url takes the chosen profile key or provider variable, never the
  default profile key. A keyless profile reads no key variable. No provider uses another
  provider key. The base URL and account are one setting, taken whole from the highest
  source.

  On cloudflare an account comes from the profile, CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID or a base URL
  holding it. --model overrides the chosen profile or request body.
  TYPESAFE_DEFAULT_MODEL applies when none names a model and beats the model in the
  default profile. A custom base URL needs no key and sends none when none resolves.
  Plain HTTP to a host other than this machine needs --allow-http, which permits sending
  the request and any API key over it. Without that flag the run exits 2 before a
  request. A profile storing such a URL needs --allow-http on every run too.

  Create a connection:
    onesie profile create NAME --provider typesafe

Execution

Flags:
      --print-request           Write API shaped request bodies to stdout and exit.
      --resume                  Carry on after the last complete line in the --out file.
                                Streaming only. Under --id it skips the ids the file
                                answers, so a record whose content changed but whose id
                                did not keeps its old answer. It then rewrites the file
                                in input order, with the answered ids the input no
                                longer has kept after them, which --prune drops.
      --prune                   Drop the answered ids the input no longer has. Needs
                                --resume and --id.
      --unordered               Emit records as they complete. Streaming only.
      --stop-on VERDICTS        End the run at the first record with one of these
                                verdicts: error, fail or unsure, comma separated. An
                                error exits with its own code. Streaming only.
      --no-dedup                Ask every record even when its request repeats an
                                earlier one. Streaming only.
      --long-context            Raise the context limits to 262k tokens, slower and less
                                accurate. Also ONESIE_LONG_CONTEXT=1. Experimental on
                                TypeSafe's side.
      --mock FILE               Answer from this file instead of the API. Also
                                ONESIE_MOCK. The file is an object keyed by question id,
                                as {"answer":0.9,"team":"billing"}, or one line per
                                record in the shape -o json writes. A lone line that
                                carries id, line, model, usage, assert, abstain or an
                                error object is one record.
      --cache                   Store responses on disk and answer a repeated request
                                from them. Also ONESIE_CACHE=1.
  -j, --jobs N                  Records in flight at once. Default: 1.
      --timeout SECONDS         Seconds per attempt, 60 for a request with images unless
                                given. Default: 10.
      --retries N               Retries after a failed attempt. Default: 2.
      --max-retry-after SECONDS
                                Most seconds to wait out a server Retry-After. Default:
                                60.

How they work together:
  --jobs bounds records in flight. --timeout is seconds per attempt. Requests with
  images default to 60 seconds unless given. Retries happen after failed attempts,
  bounded by --retries. --max-retry-after caps seconds spent waiting for a server
  Retry-After. --long-context raises context limits, is slower and less accurate, and is
  experimental on TypeSafe's side. version reports the built in limits.

  --cache, or ONESIE_CACHE=1, stores successful responses on disk and reuses repeated
  requests. onesie cache reports entries and onesie cache clear empties them. Streams
  normally preserve input order. --unordered emits completed records immediately.
  --no-dedup asks every record even when its request repeats, independently of the disk
  cache. --stop-on=error ends at the first error with its exit code, or 6 for an
  unreadable record.

  --resume needs --out. Without --id, a stream continues after the last complete line.
  With --id it skips answered ids. A record whose content changes but whose id does not
  keeps its old answer. The file is rewritten in input order. Answered ids absent from
  input stay after them unless --prune drops them.

  --mock FILE, or ONESIE_MOCK=FILE, answers from fixtures without a key or network, so
  gate scripts can be tested for free. A mock file is one JSON object keyed by question
  id, which answers every record, as in {"urgent":0.9}, or one line per record in the
  shape -o json writes, so the --out file of a real run replays. Lines match records by
  id under --id and by position otherwise. Blank lines are refused, apart from those at
  the end. An entry {"error":503} makes the record an error, as that status does,
  {"error":"timeout"} as a timeout and {"error":"connection"} as a dropped connection.
  Each is retried as a live failure is, without the wait. --print-request writes API
  shaped request bodies and sends none. Question export writes configuration rather than
  answers. Mocks exercise execution and policy behavior. Request previews and question
  export do not execute those policies. Mocks are skipped beside --list-models, request
  input, --print-request and --print-questions.

Exit codes:
  0    answered
  1    fail: an --assert was false and no --abstain-if held, or under -q alone the
       answer was judged no
  2    usage or validation error, including a missing key on the provider's own base
       URL, or a plain http base URL to another host without --allow-http
  3    the key was refused, the account is out of credits, the keychain failed, or
       profiles.json or credentials.json is exposed
  4    the server did not answer after retries, or it answered 200 with a response
       onesie could not use, which is never retried
  5    transport error or timeout
  6    a stream finished with one or more errors, or --stop-on=error stopped it at a
       record that never became a request
  7    unsure: an --assert was false and --abstain-if held, so the gate could not decide
  130  interrupted
